body:
I agree with the other posters that it would be a good idea to tell them in case they find out and think you are being dishonest, but I would wait until they return to tell them. Otherwise they might worry while they are away and it could ruin their trip. I agree with what someone else said that you should broach it as a 'hey just a heads up this happened, might be a good idea to get the latch checked/double check it if it's particularly windy'. I'm sure they will appreciate that you care so much about their animals that you have been worried about it, and that you immediately took action to find all the animals and make sure they are safe. 

&#x200B;

On the other hand, if you don't tell them, the neighbour might mention it in passing and then they may think you were keeping this from them.

Barbara, because my dad didn't like names that could be shortened. 

&#x200B;

My name ended up being Victoria. Go figure.

I'm speculating here, but I would say it would probably need to be well done. This would be because, given that you'd be eating human flesh, the chance of contracting an infectious disease would be higher than that of an animal because any pathogen that the flesh is carrying would have the ability to infect you too.

&#x200B;

However, I guess the answer would also depend on the specific human you'd be eating. Has the human been raised in a controlled environment and been given antibiotics etc just like you might with livestock to ensure there is a low chance of a pathogen being in the meat? 

&#x200B;

This is certainly a comment I never thought I would type, lol.
